{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Internet Protocol.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Internet Protocol.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"internet protocol internet protocol","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Internet Protocol.md","fileName":"Internet Protocol.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Internet Protocol","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 21:06\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Internet Infrastructure History]] [[TCP UDP and Internet Protocol Addressing]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Internet Infrastructure History","TCP UDP and Internet Protocol Addressing","Transmission Control Protocol","User Datagram Protocol","computing.epub","gameprogrammingalgorithmsandtechniques.pdf","hands-onnetworkprogrammingwithcandnetcore.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Internet Protocol.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Internet Protocol.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"internet protocol internet protocol","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Internet Protocol.md","fileName":"Internet Protocol.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Internet Protocol","heading":"Internet Protocol","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Internet Protocol addresses and routes packets from a source host toward a destination across interconnected networks. Each packet carries enough header information for routers to forward it independently.\n\nIP provides best-effort delivery rather than guaranteeing arrival or order. Higher-level protocols such as [[Transmission Control Protocol]] add reliability when an application requires it.\n\nFor a networked game, IP supplies the addressed packet layer beneath transports such as TCP and [[User Datagram Protocol]]. IPv4 and IPv6 use different address sizes, so application code should avoid assuming one fixed endpoint representation.","tag